<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Senegambia</h1><p class="st-meta">1765 CE – 1783 CE · capital Saint-Louis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's first crown colony in Africa, created after the capture of French Saint-Louis and lost again during the American War of Independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>British forces captured the French post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a> in 1758 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 left it in British hands, and in 1765 Parliament created the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a>, joining Saint-Louis with the British post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunta-kinteh-island">Fort James</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> river. It was the first British crown colony on the African continent.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a> ruled a small garrison, merchants and the town's Afro-French population. The colony existed mainly to control the gum arabic trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> river, valuable for textile dyeing, and to profit from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. The first governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ohara">Charles O'Hara</a>, quarrelled with traders and local rulers, and disease took a heavy toll on the garrison.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a> in 1779 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American War of Independence</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> of 1783 restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> to France as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a> and left <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a>, which later became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gambia-colony-and-protectorate">Gambia Colony and Protectorate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-senegambia.jpg" alt="Province of Senegambia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original upload by Nightstallion · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Senegal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1765 CE – 1783 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Louis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles O'Hara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, French, Wolof</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a>, <a href="/countries/gambia">Gambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Senegambia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1774&amp;focus=province-of-senegambia" aria-label="Province of Senegambia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4214 1632 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of Senegambia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4534 1888L4997 651"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4534 1888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4534 1888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1774&amp;focus=province-of-senegambia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

